  "schema": "graph-language-repair-question-plans-v1",
  "comment": "Declarative hop sequences executed by questions/select.py. Each hop issues exactly one core.search_rows triple with exactly one '?'-asked position and binds its result candidates under 'bind'. '{name}' in a triple is substituted from a prior bind's candidate value (via 'from') or from a question slot (via 'slot'). 'optional' means: a hop with no matches does not cancel the plan, it just leaves that bind empty. 'intersect' means: keep only candidate values present in both named binds, merging provenance from both sides. A plan with no value slot available from the question cannot be anchored to one event without a second asked position in the same triple, which the engine does not support here; select.py reports that explicitly as the 'no_value_slot' gate rather than guessing an event. 'polarity_gate' handles a negated checking action: core.search_rows has no match at all for a checking_outcome row whose own polarity is false (a negative-polarity fact is not retrievable by a plain positive triple query in this engine), but it does expose a queryable synthetic '{event}, polarity, \"true\"|\"false\"' row (verified against the real core 2026-11-11; not yet in CONTRACT.md section 4 -- flagged to model A). select.py checks this bind before the normal case_priority walk and, only when its value is the gate's 'negated_value', reports the gate's named 'case' with the fixed value \"action_not_performed\" instead of falling through to a generic no-answer hold. Predicate names elsewhere are from CONTRACT.md section 4. AUDITOR.md review 8 repairs, both generic and applied wherever a plan declares 'function_to_predicate' (source_question, occurrence_question, event_role_question): after the value_hop anchors on one function, select.py existence-checks every OTHER function the question also named (present in slots, found in the same table) against the SAME candidate event -- a question naming two role constraints that point at two different events now intersects to nothing (gate 'conflicting_constraints'), not silently answering from the anchor alone (review 8 item 1). A plan's 'required_action', when set, is existence-checked the same way against '[event, \"action\", <key>]' (review 8 item 2) -- event_role_question computes it from graphs/core/actions.json's own event_nouns (questions/compile.py.implied_actions), checking_role_question takes it directly from its cue's declared 'action'. 'merge_event_polarity_into_answer' names an optional bind (an '[event, \"polarity\", ...]' hop) whose value, when \"false\", is folded into the answer_bind's own candidates' polarities (review 8 item 3): a role value is not, by itself, evidence that its event occurred, so a negated event's role answer must carry that negation into its own provenance, not just the verification-style checking rows.",
